1
00:00:00,680 --> 00:00:03,958
There is a prison in El Salvador where the

2
00:00:03,978 --> 00:00:05,424
lights never go off.

3
00:00:05,444 --> 00:00:07,623
Where the beds have no mattresses.

4
00:00:07,643 --> 00:00:09,455
Where, according to the government,

5
00:00:09,475 --> 00:00:12,753
prisoners leave their cell for about thirty minutes a

6
00:00:12,773 --> 00:00:13,120
day.

7
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:16,845
It was built for forty thousand men.

8
00:00:16,865 --> 00:00:19,616
The government says they will never go home.

9
00:00:21,736 --> 00:00:25,284
Almost everything we know about life inside comes from

10
00:00:25,304 --> 00:00:28,455
two sources. Guided tours run by the government.

11
00:00:28,475 --> 00:00:32,022
And the stories of a group of men who

12
00:00:32,042 --> 00:00:34,797
were sent there from the United States,

13
00:00:34,817 --> 00:00:36,382
and then, unexpectedly, released.

14
00:00:37,702 --> 00:00:40,677
This is what a day inside CECOT looks like,

15
00:00:40,697 --> 00:00:43,672
according to both. And where their stories do not

16
00:00:43,692 --> 00:00:44,005
match.

17
00:00:46,745 --> 00:00:49,298
Chapter 1. The place.

18
00:00:53,898 --> 00:00:57,779
CECOT stands for Centro de Confinamiento del Terrorismo.

19
00:00:57,799 --> 00:00:59,730
The Terrorism Confinement Center.

20
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:03,144
It sits near the town of Tecoluca,

21
00:01:03,164 --> 00:01:05,094
in central El Salvador,

22
00:01:05,114 --> 00:01:08,996
at the foot of the San Vicente volcano.

23
00:01:10,316 --> 00:01:12,929
It was built in about seven months,

24
00:01:12,949 --> 00:01:16,313
and opened at the start of twenty twenty three.

25
00:01:16,333 --> 00:01:17,818
According to official figures,

26
00:01:17,838 --> 00:01:20,450
it cost around one hundred million dollars.

27
00:01:21,770 --> 00:01:25,607
It was the centerpiece of President Nayib Bukele's war

28
00:01:25,627 --> 00:01:28,607
on gangs. Since March twenty twenty two,

29
00:01:28,627 --> 00:01:32,465
El Salvador has lived under a state of exception.

30
00:01:32,485 --> 00:01:35,893
More than eighty thousand people have been arrested.

31
00:01:37,213 --> 00:01:39,865
The prison covers twenty three hectares.

32
00:01:39,885 --> 00:01:43,872
It is surrounded by two rings of concrete walls,

33
00:01:43,892 --> 00:01:47,880
each about nine meters high and sixty centimeters thick,

34
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:50,551
two electrified fences, and nineteen watchtowers.

35
00:01:50,571 --> 00:01:54,559
According to Reuters, around six hundred soldiers and two

36
00:01:54,579 --> 00:01:57,676
hundred and fifty police officers guard it.

37
00:01:58,996 --> 00:02:01,247
Inside are eight cell blocks.

38
00:02:01,267 --> 00:02:03,064
Official capacity, forty thousand.

39
00:02:03,084 --> 00:02:05,790
How many people are actually inside,

40
00:02:05,810 --> 00:02:08,061
the government does not say.

41
00:02:08,081 --> 00:02:10,333
In June twenty twenty four,

42
00:02:10,353 --> 00:02:14,421
the figure was put at around fourteen and a

43
00:02:14,441 --> 00:02:17,147
half thousand. In twenty twenty five,

44
00:02:17,167 --> 00:02:21,236
the prison's director said it was closer to forty

45
00:02:21,256 --> 00:02:23,507
thousand. No independent count exists.

46
00:02:24,827 --> 00:02:27,145
And in April twenty twenty five,

47
00:02:27,165 --> 00:02:29,873
Bukele announced he would double its capacity,

48
00:02:29,893 --> 00:02:31,042
to eighty thousand.

49
00:02:33,182 --> 00:02:35,514
Chapter 2. Arrival.

50
00:02:40,114 --> 00:02:43,800
A day in CECOT begins long before the first

51
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:45,860
morning. It begins with arrival.

52
00:02:47,780 --> 00:02:51,684
Government videos show the same scene again and again.

53
00:02:51,704 --> 00:02:53,428
Rows of men, shackled,

54
00:02:53,448 --> 00:02:56,480
heads bowed, walked in at a crouch.

55
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:58,224
Their heads are shaved.

56
00:02:58,244 --> 00:03:01,712
They are given white shorts and white shirts.

57
00:03:01,732 --> 00:03:05,200
Every new arrival, according to journalists who visited,

58
00:03:05,220 --> 00:03:06,944
is searched and scanned.

59
00:03:08,264 --> 00:03:12,528
According to CNN, prisoners' heads are shaved again every

60
00:03:12,548 --> 00:03:13,480
five days.

61
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:18,329
The men deported from the United States in March

62
00:03:18,349 --> 00:03:21,878
twenty twenty five described their arrival in harsher terms.

63
00:03:21,898 --> 00:03:24,244
In interviews with ProPublica and FRONTLINE,

64
00:03:24,264 --> 00:03:27,792
they said Salvadoran police boarded the planes and pushed

65
00:03:27,812 --> 00:03:30,158
them off, hitting them with batons.

66
00:03:30,178 --> 00:03:33,707
They said men who resisted having their heads shaved

67
00:03:33,727 --> 00:03:34,496
were beaten.

68
00:03:35,816 --> 00:03:38,368
One of them, Juan José Ramos,

69
00:03:38,388 --> 00:03:41,796
remembered what they were told when they arrived.

70
00:03:41,816 --> 00:03:45,225
Welcome to CECOT. You're going to die here.

71
00:03:47,745 --> 00:03:50,944
El Salvador's government has denied that the men were

72
00:03:50,964 --> 00:03:51,301
mistreated.

73
00:03:53,641 --> 00:03:55,985
Chapter 3. The cell.

74
00:04:00,585 --> 00:04:01,471
Then, the cell.

75
00:04:03,391 --> 00:04:06,802
There are two hundred and fifty six of them.

76
00:04:06,822 --> 00:04:10,233
Each one a cage of steel bars inside a

77
00:04:10,253 --> 00:04:13,283
huge concrete hall. If the prison were full,

78
00:04:13,303 --> 00:04:15,189
according to a BBC analysis,

79
00:04:15,209 --> 00:04:18,620
each cell would hold around one hundred and fifty

80
00:04:18,640 --> 00:04:22,051
six men. That is about zero point six square

81
00:04:22,071 --> 00:04:25,482
meters per person. Less space than a standard single

82
00:04:25,502 --> 00:04:25,864
bed.

83
00:04:27,184 --> 00:04:30,617
Inside, the furniture is steel.

84
00:04:30,637 --> 00:04:34,070
Bunks, stacked four levels high.

85
00:04:34,090 --> 00:04:36,832
No mattresses. No sheets.

86
00:04:36,852 --> 00:04:39,594
Two toilets. Two basins.

87
00:04:39,614 --> 00:04:42,357
And, according to visitors,

88
00:04:42,377 --> 00:04:43,738
two Bibles.

89
00:04:45,058 --> 00:04:48,746
The lights stay on twenty four hours a day.

90
00:04:48,766 --> 00:04:50,393
Cameras watch the cells.

91
00:04:50,413 --> 00:04:52,865
Armed guards watch from the corridors.

92
00:04:54,185 --> 00:04:57,368
The deported men described something else the tours did

93
00:04:57,388 --> 00:05:00,570
not show. One said open sewage pipes ran through

94
00:05:00,590 --> 00:05:03,772
the cells, and that the walls were covered in

95
00:05:03,792 --> 00:05:06,975
mold. Another said the water for washing was the

96
00:05:06,995 --> 00:05:09,110
same water used for the toilets.

97
00:05:10,430 --> 00:05:12,113
Their numbers were different too.

98
00:05:12,133 --> 00:05:15,180
One of them said guards put ten to fifteen

99
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:17,906
people in each cell where they were held.

100
00:05:17,926 --> 00:05:20,973
That is far below the hundred and fifty six

101
00:05:20,993 --> 00:05:22,336
of the BBC calculation.

102
00:05:22,356 --> 00:05:25,402
It is possible that conditions in their part of

103
00:05:25,422 --> 00:05:27,787
the prison were different from the rest.

104
00:05:29,327 --> 00:05:31,718
Chapter 4. Morning.

105
00:05:36,318 --> 00:05:37,889
There is no window.

106
00:05:37,909 --> 00:05:41,471
No sunrise. According to the men who were released,

107
00:05:41,491 --> 00:05:44,257
they quickly lost track of the days,

108
00:05:44,277 --> 00:05:46,644
because they were never allowed outside.

109
00:05:48,764 --> 00:05:51,310
Ramos described the start of each day.

110
00:05:51,330 --> 00:05:54,242
Being woken up every day at four a.m.,

111
00:05:54,262 --> 00:05:56,808
he said, to be insulted and beaten.

112
00:05:56,828 --> 00:06:00,106
One man said that when he asked a guard

113
00:06:00,126 --> 00:06:01,572
what time it was,

114
00:06:01,592 --> 00:06:03,038
the guard mocked him.

115
00:06:04,358 --> 00:06:07,325
Official tours present the morning differently.

116
00:06:07,345 --> 00:06:10,809
Lines of prisoners sitting on the floor,

117
00:06:10,829 --> 00:06:12,800
in perfect rows, silent,

118
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:16,782
while guards in riot gear stand over them.

119
00:06:16,802 --> 00:06:18,275
Counts. Inspections. Order.

120
00:06:19,595 --> 00:06:22,283
Rival gang members are not separated.

121
00:06:22,303 --> 00:06:24,088
According to the government,

122
00:06:24,108 --> 00:06:28,149
members of MS thirteen and Barrio Eighteen share the

123
00:06:28,169 --> 00:06:30,857
same halls. In the outside world,

124
00:06:30,877 --> 00:06:33,565
they spent decades killing each other.

125
00:06:33,585 --> 00:06:35,821
In CECOT, the government says,

126
00:06:35,841 --> 00:06:37,626
they have no choice.

127
00:06:39,166 --> 00:06:41,092
Chapter 5. Food.

128
00:06:45,692 --> 00:06:48,041
Meals come into the cell.

129
00:06:48,061 --> 00:06:50,884
According to officials quoted by CNN,

130
00:06:50,904 --> 00:06:52,779
prisoners eat rice, beans,

131
00:06:52,799 --> 00:06:54,201
eggs and pasta.

132
00:06:55,521 --> 00:06:57,648
There are no knives.

133
00:06:57,668 --> 00:06:59,796
No forks. No spoons.

134
00:06:59,816 --> 00:07:01,943
They could become weapons.

135
00:07:01,963 --> 00:07:05,165
So prisoners eat with their hands.

136
00:07:07,285 --> 00:07:10,549
The released men said that most days they got

137
00:07:10,569 --> 00:07:12,009
beans, rice and tortillas.

138
00:07:12,029 --> 00:07:15,294
One of them said the food tasted like soap.

139
00:07:16,614 --> 00:07:19,180
At least twice, according to ProPublica,

140
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:22,197
the Venezuelan prisoners went on hunger strike,

141
00:07:22,217 --> 00:07:25,644
refusing meals for several days at a time,

142
00:07:25,664 --> 00:07:27,368
to protest their detention.

143
00:07:28,908 --> 00:07:31,786
Chapter 6. Thirty minutes.

144
00:07:36,386 --> 00:07:38,844
For most of the day,

145
00:07:38,864 --> 00:07:41,322
there is nothing. No work.

146
00:07:41,342 --> 00:07:43,304
No classes. No visits.

147
00:07:43,324 --> 00:07:46,773
No phone calls, according to the BBC.

148
00:07:48,093 --> 00:07:51,930
According to CNN, prisoners leave the cell only for

149
00:07:51,950 --> 00:07:54,930
a few reasons. Thirty minutes of exercise,

150
00:07:54,950 --> 00:07:57,073
in the hallway, under guard.

151
00:07:57,093 --> 00:07:58,788
Bible study. A hearing,

152
00:07:58,808 --> 00:08:00,502
held by video link,

153
00:08:00,522 --> 00:08:04,360
so that they never have to leave the prison

154
00:08:04,380 --> 00:08:06,503
for court. Or solitary confinement.

155
00:08:07,823 --> 00:08:09,545
According to the released men,

156
00:08:09,565 --> 00:08:11,288
they were never allowed outside.

157
00:08:11,308 --> 00:08:13,380
The sky, for the men inside,

158
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:16,517
may be something they do not see for years.

159
00:08:18,637 --> 00:08:20,736
Showers follow the guards' schedule.

160
00:08:20,756 --> 00:08:24,551
One of the deported men said he was beaten

161
00:08:24,571 --> 00:08:27,941
for trying to wash outside the allowed time.

162
00:08:27,961 --> 00:08:31,756
Another described guards ordering everyone to shower just after

163
00:08:31,776 --> 00:08:32,180
midnight.

164
00:08:33,720 --> 00:08:35,959
Chapter 7. The island.

165
00:08:40,559 --> 00:08:42,711
And for those who break the rules,

166
00:08:42,731 --> 00:08:45,504
there is a place the prisoners reportedly call the

167
00:08:45,524 --> 00:08:46,746
island. Or the hole.

168
00:08:48,866 --> 00:08:52,203
According to CNN, solitary cells have a concrete bed,

169
00:08:52,223 --> 00:08:54,069
a toilet and a basin,

170
00:08:54,089 --> 00:08:57,427
and a single small opening in the ceiling for

171
00:08:57,447 --> 00:09:00,784
light. Prisoners can be held there for up to

172
00:09:00,804 --> 00:09:01,531
fifteen days.

173
00:09:02,851 --> 00:09:06,030
One of the Venezuelans told FRONTLINE he was held

174
00:09:06,050 --> 00:09:07,807
in it more than once.

175
00:09:07,827 --> 00:09:09,939
He described it as completely sealed,

176
00:09:09,959 --> 00:09:12,427
with only a tiny ray of light.

177
00:09:14,747 --> 00:09:19,323
The released men described beatings that were random and

178
00:09:19,343 --> 00:09:21,365
constant. Fists, batons, boots.

179
00:09:21,385 --> 00:09:24,429
Rubber pellets fired at close range.

180
00:09:24,449 --> 00:09:29,024
Ramos believes one beating cost him the sight in

181
00:09:29,044 --> 00:09:31,577
one eye. These are allegations.

182
00:09:31,597 --> 00:09:35,152
El Salvador's vice president denied any mistreatment,

183
00:09:35,172 --> 00:09:39,747
and pointed to videos of the men leaving apparently

184
00:09:39,767 --> 00:09:40,258
unharmed.

185
00:09:41,578 --> 00:09:45,107
After about three and a half months,

186
00:09:45,127 --> 00:09:47,643
the men said, conditions improved.

187
00:09:47,663 --> 00:09:49,671
They received painkillers, antibiotics,

188
00:09:49,691 --> 00:09:53,221
toothbrushes, and a visit from a psychologist.

189
00:09:54,761 --> 00:09:56,838
Chapter 8. Night.

190
00:10:01,438 --> 00:10:04,356
Night in CECOT does not look different from day.

191
00:10:04,376 --> 00:10:05,662
The lights stay on.

192
00:10:07,782 --> 00:10:09,908
The men sleep on steel.

193
00:10:09,928 --> 00:10:11,625
In a full cell,

194
00:10:11,645 --> 00:10:13,771
according to the official design,

195
00:10:13,791 --> 00:10:16,346
shoulder to shoulder, four levels high.

196
00:10:16,366 --> 00:10:17,633
Cameras keep recording.

197
00:10:18,953 --> 00:10:22,932
And they wait. Many have not been convicted of

198
00:10:22,952 --> 00:10:26,931
anything. In El Salvador's mass trials under the state

199
00:10:26,951 --> 00:10:30,930
of exception, cases are often processed in large groups,

200
00:10:30,950 --> 00:10:34,040
sometimes hundreds of defendants at a time.

201
00:10:35,360 --> 00:10:38,736
The prison's director has described the inmates as the

202
00:10:38,756 --> 00:10:40,246
worst of the worst.

203
00:10:40,266 --> 00:10:43,643
The justice minister has said they will never return

204
00:10:43,663 --> 00:10:45,152
to their communities. Rehabilitation,

205
00:10:45,172 --> 00:10:48,171
he said, is not part of the plan.

206
00:10:50,511 --> 00:10:53,215
Chapter 9. The ones who left.

207
00:10:57,815 --> 00:10:59,789
For most prisoners in CECOT,

208
00:10:59,809 --> 00:11:01,783
there is no way out.

209
00:11:01,803 --> 00:11:03,776
But in twenty twenty five,

210
00:11:03,796 --> 00:11:05,372
one group did leave.

211
00:11:07,492 --> 00:11:09,825
On March fifteenth, twenty twenty five,

212
00:11:09,845 --> 00:11:13,355
the United States flew more than two hundred Venezuelan

213
00:11:13,375 --> 00:11:16,884
men to El Salvador under the Alien Enemies Act,

214
00:11:16,904 --> 00:11:19,238
a law from seventeen ninety eight.

215
00:11:19,258 --> 00:11:22,767
The U.S. government said they were members of the

216
00:11:22,787 --> 00:11:24,336
Tren de Aragua gang.

217
00:11:25,656 --> 00:11:27,198
ProPublica later reviewed U.S.

218
00:11:27,218 --> 00:11:30,713
government data on two hundred and thirty eight of

219
00:11:30,733 --> 00:11:34,228
the men. It found that at least one hundred

220
00:11:34,248 --> 00:11:37,743
and ninety seven had no criminal convictions in the

221
00:11:37,763 --> 00:11:39,695
United States. Many had tattoos,

222
00:11:39,715 --> 00:11:43,210
which officials had treated as signs of gang membership.

223
00:11:43,230 --> 00:11:46,334
The administration maintained that many were gang members.

224
00:11:47,654 --> 00:11:50,970
On the same flights was a Salvadoran man,

225
00:11:50,990 --> 00:11:54,722
Kilmar Abrego Garcia, who had been living in Maryland.

226
00:11:54,742 --> 00:11:58,474
The U.S. government later admitted in court that his

227
00:11:58,494 --> 00:12:00,559
deportation was an administrative error.

228
00:12:00,579 --> 00:12:02,643
In April twenty twenty five,

229
00:12:02,663 --> 00:12:06,396
the U.S. Supreme Court said the government should facilitate

230
00:12:06,416 --> 00:12:07,230
his return.

231
00:12:08,550 --> 00:12:10,717
For weeks, it did not happen.

232
00:12:10,737 --> 00:12:12,904
Then, in June twenty twenty five,

233
00:12:12,924 --> 00:12:16,184
Abrego Garcia was brought back to the United States,

234
00:12:16,204 --> 00:12:18,007
to face criminal charges there,

235
00:12:18,027 --> 00:12:21,287
which he denied. His case became the most famous

236
00:12:21,307 --> 00:12:24,568
example of how hard it is to get anyone

237
00:12:24,588 --> 00:12:27,119
out of CECOT, once they are in.

238
00:12:28,439 --> 00:12:30,895
On July eighteenth, twenty twenty five,

239
00:12:30,915 --> 00:12:34,195
the Venezuelans were released and flown to Venezuela,

240
00:12:34,215 --> 00:12:35,845
in a prisoner exchange.

241
00:12:35,865 --> 00:12:39,557
Photographers were allowed into the prison as they left.

242
00:12:39,577 --> 00:12:43,270
It is the main reason we have their testimony

243
00:12:43,290 --> 00:12:44,095
at all.

244
00:12:45,415 --> 00:12:47,039
One of them, Andry Blanco,

245
00:12:47,059 --> 00:12:48,683
put it in one sentence.

246
00:12:48,703 --> 00:12:51,642
We are a group of people who I consider

247
00:12:51,662 --> 00:12:54,601
had the bad luck of ending up on this

248
00:12:54,621 --> 00:12:55,259
black list.

249
00:12:57,799 --> 00:13:00,236
Chapter 10. The showroom.

250
00:13:04,836 --> 00:13:08,605
Since then, CECOT has become something else.

251
00:13:08,625 --> 00:13:11,313
A destination for foreign politicians.

252
00:13:12,633 --> 00:13:15,430
In January twenty twenty six,

253
00:13:15,450 --> 00:13:18,248
Chile's president, José Antonio Kast,

254
00:13:18,268 --> 00:13:21,065
toured the prison. In October,

255
00:13:21,085 --> 00:13:23,883
Costa Rica's president, Laura Fernández,

256
00:13:23,903 --> 00:13:26,700
came with her security team.

257
00:13:26,720 --> 00:13:30,082
Peru's Keiko Fujimori reportedly visited too.

258
00:13:30,102 --> 00:13:35,153
And in November, as Salvadoran soldiers fought gangs in

259
00:13:35,173 --> 00:13:39,661
Haiti, Haiti's justice minister was reportedly shown around.

260
00:13:40,981 --> 00:13:43,962
Each visitor sees the same things.

261
00:13:43,982 --> 00:13:46,462
The rows of white uniforms.

262
00:13:46,482 --> 00:13:49,463
The steel bunks. The bright corridors.

263
00:13:49,483 --> 00:13:51,463
The message is clear.

264
00:13:51,483 --> 00:13:54,464
This is what order looks like.

265
00:13:56,584 --> 00:13:58,735
Not everyone gets the tour.

266
00:13:58,755 --> 00:14:00,907
In April twenty twenty five,

267
00:14:00,927 --> 00:14:02,645
a United States senator,

268
00:14:02,665 --> 00:14:06,554
Chris Van Hollen, traveled to El Salvador to check

269
00:14:06,574 --> 00:14:10,463
on Abrego Garcia. He was not allowed into CECOT.

270
00:14:11,783 --> 00:14:15,571
What the visitors do not see is just as

271
00:14:15,591 --> 00:14:19,379
important. We could not find any public report of

272
00:14:19,399 --> 00:14:23,187
an independent inspection of CECOT by the United Nations

273
00:14:23,207 --> 00:14:24,880
or the Red Cross.

274
00:14:24,900 --> 00:14:27,842
Journalists come in only on guided tours.

275
00:14:27,862 --> 00:14:30,380
Lawyers, families and outside observers are,

276
00:14:30,400 --> 00:14:32,496
by most accounts, kept out.

277
00:14:33,816 --> 00:14:37,848
So the day inside CECOT exists in two versions.

278
00:14:37,868 --> 00:14:40,549
The government's, clean, silent and controlled.

279
00:14:40,569 --> 00:14:44,601
And the one told by the few men who

280
00:14:44,621 --> 00:14:48,652
walked out. The truth is somewhere behind those walls.

281
00:14:48,672 --> 00:14:52,704
And for now, almost nobody outside is allowed to

282
00:14:52,724 --> 00:14:53,154
check.

283
00:14:55,674 --> 00:14:57,597
Which version do you believe?

284
00:14:57,617 --> 00:14:59,539
Tell us in the comments.

285
00:14:59,559 --> 00:15:03,035
And to see where else Bukele's methods are spreading,

286
00:15:03,055 --> 00:15:06,531
watch our story about El Salvador's soldiers in Haiti,

